1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,016
Lesson 3.3.

2
00:00:02,396 --> 00:00:03,785
Fluctuation theorems:

3
00:00:04,165 --> 00:00:05,460
Crooks and Jarzynski.

4
00:00:06,583 --> 00:00:11,159
For a century, measuring a
free-energy difference required an

5
00:00:11,159 --> 00:00:16,185
infinitely slow process, because
only then is the work equal to ΔF.

6
00:00:16,565 --> 00:00:21,245
In 1997 Christopher Jarzynski
showed something surprising:

7
00:00:21,245 --> 00:00:25,280
ΔF can be obtained exactly
from fast, irreversible

8
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:29,556
processes, if we average
the exponential of the work.

9
00:00:29,936 --> 00:00:32,546
Two years later, Gavin
Crooks gave the detailed

10
00:00:32,546 --> 00:00:35,377
relation between the forward
and reverse processes.

11
00:00:36,483 --> 00:00:39,211
We stiffen a harmonic trap: the stiffness

12
00:00:39,211 --> 00:00:41,806
goes from one to four in half a second.

13
00:00:42,186 --> 00:00:47,248
Since the stiffer trap
confines the particle more, the

14
00:00:47,248 --> 00:00:51,559
free energy rises by ½
ln 4, about 0.69 k_B T.

15
00:00:51,939 --> 00:00:56,619
But the process is fast: the
mean work is larger, 1.08.

16
00:00:56,999 --> 00:01:00,597
Some trajectories, however,
spend less than ΔF.

17
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:03,897
The Jarzynski equality weights those rare

18
00:01:03,897 --> 00:01:07,742
trajectories exactly enough
to recover the true value.

19
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:15,182
The equality says: ⟨e^(−βW)⟩ = e^(−βΔF).

20
00:01:15,562 --> 00:01:19,491
The average is over trajectories
that start at equilibrium with the

21
00:01:19,491 --> 00:01:23,126
initial parameter; the final
state need not be at equilibrium.

22
00:01:23,506 --> 00:01:25,263
It is a consequence of the integral

23
00:01:25,263 --> 00:01:27,522
fluctuation theorem from
the previous lesson.

24
00:01:27,902 --> 00:01:34,034
With Jensen's inequality it
recovers the second law, ⟨W⟩ ≥ ΔF.

25
00:01:34,414 --> 00:01:39,434
And with our simulation of a
hundred thousand trajectories, the

26
00:01:39,434 --> 00:01:44,375
Jarzynski estimator gives 0.693,
versus the exact value 0.693.

27
00:01:45,483 --> 00:01:48,022
Crooks compares the
forward process with the

28
00:01:48,022 --> 00:01:50,618
reverse: softening the
trap from four to one.

29
00:01:50,998 --> 00:01:55,437
The forward work distribution,
divided by the reverse work

30
00:01:55,437 --> 00:01:59,187
distribution with flipped
sign, is e^(β(W − ΔF)).

31
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:05,445
Visible consequence: the two
histograms cross exactly at W = ΔF.

32
00:02:05,825 --> 00:02:11,988
Moreover, the free energy is bracketed
by the mean works: between minus

33
00:02:11,988 --> 00:02:17,282
the mean reverse work, 0.49,
and the mean forward work, 1.08.

34
00:02:18,383 --> 00:02:19,720
There is a practical catch.

35
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:24,642
The exponential average is dominated
by rare trajectories with low work.

36
00:02:25,022 --> 00:02:27,868
With few trajectories,
you probably have not

37
00:02:27,868 --> 00:02:30,779
seen any, and the
estimator overestimates ΔF.

38
00:02:31,159 --> 00:02:35,378
With five trajectories,
the estimator averages

39
00:02:35,378 --> 00:02:39,779
0.73; with twenty, 0.70;
with a thousand, 0.693.

40
00:02:40,159 --> 00:02:43,534
Bias and variance fall as
the number of samples grows.

41
00:02:43,914 --> 00:02:47,093
Bennett's estimator, which
uses forward and reverse

42
00:02:47,093 --> 00:02:51,082
trajectories together, has minimum
variance and needs far fewer.

43
00:02:52,183 --> 00:02:54,056
Let's summarize the
numbers of the example.

44
00:02:54,436 --> 00:02:59,690
Exact free energy: one half
of the log of four, 0.693.

45
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:06,320
Mean forward work: 1.08, that
is, 0.39 of dissipated work.

46
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,302
Minus the mean reverse work: 0.49.

47
00:03:10,682 --> 00:03:14,008
The second law holds in both
directions, and Jarzynski gets

48
00:03:14,008 --> 00:03:17,220
three digits right using a
hundred thousand trajectories.

49
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:21,941
Jarzynski and Crooks are
used in biophysics to measure

50
00:03:21,941 --> 00:03:25,332
folding free energies by
pulling molecules quickly.

51
00:03:25,712 --> 00:03:31,281
In this course we will use them to compute
the log-determinant of a matrix from

52
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:36,287
the work between two quadratic
potentials, in lab four and in unit six.

53
00:03:37,417 --> 00:03:40,172
Common mistakes: not
starting at equilibrium.

54
00:03:40,552 --> 00:03:43,500
Using few trajectories in
highly dissipative processes.

55
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:47,793
Confusing the average of the exponential
with the exponential of the average.

56
00:03:48,173 --> 00:03:50,879
And believing Jarzynski
contradicts the second law.

57
00:03:51,983 --> 00:03:52,816
In summary:

58
00:03:53,196 --> 00:04:02,558
Jarzynski gives the exact free energy
from fast processes, ⟨e^(−βW)⟩ = e^(−βΔF).

59
00:04:02,938 --> 00:04:08,609
Crooks relates the forward and reverse
works, whose distributions cross at ΔF.

60
00:04:08,989 --> 00:04:12,311
In practice, the estimator
has a bias that decreases with

61
00:04:12,311 --> 00:04:15,282
the number of trajectories,
and Bennett reduces it.

62
00:04:16,383 --> 00:04:21,990
To practice: compute the free energy of
stiffening a trap, prove that Crooks implies

63
00:04:21,990 --> 00:04:26,729
Jarzynski, apply the Gaussian formula
and explain the estimator's bias.
